1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,884
In 2020, Tesla finally did something it had never

2
00:00:03,884 --> 00:00:07,249
done before: it made a profit for a full year.

3
00:00:07,249 --> 00:00:10,540
Seven hundred and twenty-one million dollars.

4
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:15,499
That same year, it collected one billion, five hundred and eighty million

5
00:00:15,499 --> 00:00:20,070
dollars from a source that has nothing to do with selling cars to drivers.

6
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:23,159
The money came from other carmakers.

7
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:27,629
Companies like Stellantis, Toyota, Ford and GM, paying Tesla,

8
00:00:27,629 --> 00:00:32,760
year after year, for something that cost Tesla almost nothing to make.

9
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:36,273
So the most valuable car company in the world became

10
00:00:36,273 --> 00:00:39,276
profitable thanks to cheques written by its rivals.

11
00:00:39,276 --> 00:00:42,810
And that's only the first strange thing in Tesla's accounts.

12
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:46,460
Let's open them up, and follow every dollar.

13
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:53,175
First, a reminder of where Tesla comes from.

14
00:00:53,175 --> 00:00:55,724
For most of its life, it lost money.

15
00:00:55,724 --> 00:00:58,769
Between 2016 and 2019 alone, its operations

16
00:00:58,769 --> 00:01:02,096
lost more than two point seven billion dollars.

17
00:01:02,096 --> 00:01:03,300
Profit is recent.

18
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:09,544
When most people think about Tesla, they think about one thing: electric cars.

19
00:01:09,544 --> 00:01:13,630
But the company actually reports four kinds of revenue.

20
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:19,640
In 2025, Tesla brought in about ninety-five billion dollars.

21
00:01:20,090 --> 00:01:24,672
Around seventy billion came from cars: selling them, leasing them,

22
00:01:24,672 --> 00:01:29,810
and that strange line called "regulatory credits" that we'll come back to.

23
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:33,507
Almost thirteen billion came from energy:

24
00:01:33,507 --> 00:01:38,100
giant batteries for power grids, home batteries and solar.

25
00:01:38,550 --> 00:01:44,897
And about twelve and a half billion came from what Tesla calls "services and other":

26
00:01:44,897 --> 00:01:50,110
charging at its Supercharger stations, repairs, insurance, used cars.

27
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:54,398
Ninety-five billion dollars sounds enormous, and it is.

28
00:01:54,398 --> 00:01:59,911
But 2025 was also the first year in Tesla's history when its revenue went down.

29
00:01:59,911 --> 00:02:03,400
To understand why, we need to start with the cars.

30
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:09,300
For a decade, Tesla's story was simple: every year, more cars.

31
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:12,363
Half a million deliveries in 2020.

32
00:02:12,363 --> 00:02:15,590
More than one point eight million in 2023.

33
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:17,885
Then the curve bent.

34
00:02:17,885 --> 00:02:21,298
One point seven nine million in 2024.

35
00:02:21,298 --> 00:02:26,280
One point six four million in 2025, down nine percent.

36
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,173
Part of the reason is competition.

37
00:02:29,173 --> 00:02:33,628
In 2025, the Chinese company BYD sold about two point two five

38
00:02:33,628 --> 00:02:38,084
million fully electric cars, and took the title of the world's

39
00:02:38,084 --> 00:02:42,970
biggest seller of electric vehicles away from Tesla for a full year.

40
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:46,208
There's also the question of the lineup.

41
00:02:46,208 --> 00:02:49,903
Tesla's sales now rest almost entirely on two models.

42
00:02:49,903 --> 00:02:55,340
In the third quarter of 2026, the Model 3 and Model Y made up four hundred and

43
00:02:55,340 --> 00:03:00,777
seventy-eight thousand of its four hundred and eighty-six thousand deliveries.

44
00:03:00,777 --> 00:03:05,239
Everything else, including the Cybertruck and the Model S and X,

45
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:09,700
sold about eight thousand units, half as many as a year earlier.

46
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:14,414
To keep selling, Tesla cut its prices, again and again.

47
00:03:14,414 --> 00:03:18,368
You can see the effect in one number: gross margin,

48
00:03:18,368 --> 00:03:23,950
the share of each sale left after paying for parts, labor and factories.

49
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:28,281
In 2022, Tesla kept about twenty-six cents

50
00:03:28,281 --> 00:03:32,346
of gross profit for every dollar of revenue.

51
00:03:32,346 --> 00:03:37,520
By 2024 and 2025, that was down to about eighteen cents.

52
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:40,474
That's still better than many carmakers.

53
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:44,856
But it means each car sold brings in a lot less money than it used to.

54
00:03:44,856 --> 00:03:49,050
And it makes that strange line in the accounts even more important.

55
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:51,230
Here's how it works.

56
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:56,124
Governments, in places like California, the European Union and China,

57
00:03:56,124 --> 00:03:59,086
set limits on how much their cars can pollute,

58
00:03:59,086 --> 00:04:01,920
on average, across a carmaker's whole range.

59
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:06,824
A company that sells lots of big gasoline vehicles may go over the limit.

60
00:04:06,824 --> 00:04:09,204
It then has two options: pay a penalty,

61
00:04:09,204 --> 00:04:13,170
or buy credits from a company that's comfortably under the limit.

62
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:17,039
And no one is more comfortably under the limit

63
00:04:17,039 --> 00:04:20,383
than a company that only sells electric cars.

64
00:04:20,383 --> 00:04:22,984
Every Tesla sold generates credits.

65
00:04:22,984 --> 00:04:25,660
Tesla sells them to its competitors.

66
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:30,702
The key point: producing those credits costs Tesla almost nothing.

67
00:04:30,702 --> 00:04:33,346
The cars were going to be sold anyway.

68
00:04:33,346 --> 00:04:39,190
So this revenue is, in the words of one industry newspaper, "virtually pure profit".

69
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,364
The amounts grew fast.

70
00:04:41,364 --> 00:04:44,813
About three hundred million dollars in 2016.

71
00:04:44,813 --> 00:04:47,713
One point five eight billion in 2020.

72
00:04:47,713 --> 00:04:51,240
A record two point seven six billion in 2024.

73
00:04:51,690 --> 00:04:58,770
Added up since 2009, Tesla has collected almost fourteen billion dollars this way.

74
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:01,525
Now put that next to its profits.

75
00:05:01,525 --> 00:05:07,113
In 2020, the credits were more than twice the size of Tesla's entire net profit.

76
00:05:07,113 --> 00:05:12,492
In 2025, Tesla's credits brought in around two billion dollars, almost all of

77
00:05:12,492 --> 00:05:17,940
it profit, while the company's total net income was three point eight billion.

78
00:05:18,390 --> 00:05:23,927
So in its first profitable year, and again in 2025, a big share of what

79
00:05:23,927 --> 00:05:29,230
Tesla kept came, indirectly, from other carmakers' gasoline engines.

80
00:05:30,330 --> 00:05:34,010
That source of money is now drying up, at least in the United States.

81
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:38,800
In July 2025, a new law in the US, nicknamed the "One Big

82
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:43,065
Beautiful Bill", cut back support for electric vehicles.

83
00:05:43,065 --> 00:05:47,405
The seven thousand five hundred dollar federal tax credit

84
00:05:47,405 --> 00:05:51,212
for buying one ended on September thirtieth, 2025.

85
00:05:51,212 --> 00:05:56,619
And the federal regulator stopped enforcing its fuel economy penalties.

86
00:05:56,619 --> 00:06:01,340
When nobody has to pay a penalty, nobody needs to buy credits.

87
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:04,652
The result shows up in Tesla's own numbers.

88
00:06:04,652 --> 00:06:07,912
In the second quarter of 2026, its credit revenue

89
00:06:07,912 --> 00:06:10,974
was one hundred and forty-six million dollars.

90
00:06:10,974 --> 00:06:14,967
A year earlier, it was four hundred and thirty-nine million.

91
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:16,830
That's a drop of two-thirds.

92
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,731
The end of the tax credit also distorted car sales.

93
00:06:20,731 --> 00:06:25,400
American buyers rushed to get their seven and a half thousand dollars

94
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:29,798
before the deadline, which made the third quarter of 2025 Tesla's

95
00:06:29,798 --> 00:06:34,061
best quarter ever: four hundred and ninety-seven thousand cars.

96
00:06:34,061 --> 00:06:39,000
A year later, without that rush, deliveries were about two percent lower.

97
00:06:39,450 --> 00:06:43,378
Credits are still sold in other markets, like Europe and China.

98
00:06:43,378 --> 00:06:46,994
But one of Tesla's easiest sources of profit is shrinking.

99
00:06:46,994 --> 00:06:50,610
Which raises the obvious question: what's growing instead?

100
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:53,110
The answer isn't a car.

101
00:06:53,560 --> 00:06:58,914
Tesla builds huge battery systems, each one about the size of a shipping container,

102
00:06:58,914 --> 00:07:04,009
that power companies use to store electricity and release it when demand peaks.

103
00:07:04,009 --> 00:07:05,880
It also sells home batteries.

104
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:12,074
In 2020, Tesla installed about three gigawatt-hours of storage.

105
00:07:12,074 --> 00:07:14,810
In 2024, more than thirty-one.

106
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:17,363
In 2025, almost forty-seven.

107
00:07:17,363 --> 00:07:21,010
That's fifteen times more in five years.

108
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:24,800
Revenue followed: from two billion dollars

109
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,220
in 2020 to almost thirteen billion in 2025.

110
00:07:28,670 --> 00:07:32,260
And this business is more profitable than the cars.

111
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:36,553
In 2024, Tesla kept about twenty-six cents of gross profit on

112
00:07:36,553 --> 00:07:40,988
each dollar of energy sales, and close to thirty cents in 2025,

113
00:07:40,988 --> 00:07:45,070
compared with roughly eighteen for the company as a whole.

114
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:48,380
It's not the part of Tesla that makes headlines.

115
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:51,120
But it's the part that's been growing fastest.

116
00:07:52,220 --> 00:07:54,300
The third business is quieter still.

117
00:07:54,750 --> 00:08:00,619
By the end of 2024, Tesla operated almost seven thousand Supercharger stations,

118
00:08:00,619 --> 00:08:04,704
with more than sixty-five thousand charging connectors.

119
00:08:04,704 --> 00:08:08,121
Every time a driver plugs in, Tesla gets paid.

120
00:08:08,121 --> 00:08:13,841
Add repairs, insurance, parts and used cars, and "services and other" brought

121
00:08:13,841 --> 00:08:19,190
in about twelve and a half billion dollars in 2025, up nineteen percent.

122
00:08:19,640 --> 00:08:22,932
The more Teslas there are on the road, the more this

123
00:08:22,932 --> 00:08:26,160
line grows, even in a year when new car sales fall.

124
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:29,480
So that's where the money comes from.

125
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:30,500
Where does it go?

126
00:08:30,950 --> 00:08:34,388
First, into building cars and batteries: after those costs,

127
00:08:34,388 --> 00:08:38,059
only about eighteen percent of revenue is left as gross profit.

128
00:08:38,059 --> 00:08:41,555
Then into research, salaries, sales and administration for a

129
00:08:41,555 --> 00:08:45,110
company of about one hundred and thirty-five thousand people.

130
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:47,913
What's left has been shrinking.

131
00:08:47,913 --> 00:08:52,770
Leaving aside a one-time tax benefit in 2023, Tesla's net profit

132
00:08:52,770 --> 00:08:57,172
peaked at about twelve and a half billion dollars in 2022.

133
00:08:57,172 --> 00:09:00,360
In 2025, it was three point eight billion.

134
00:09:00,810 --> 00:09:05,310
Meanwhile, spending on new factories and equipment kept climbing:

135
00:09:05,310 --> 00:09:10,572
three point two billion dollars in 2020, eleven point three billion in 2024.

136
00:09:10,572 --> 00:09:14,173
In 2024, after paying for all of it, Tesla still had

137
00:09:14,173 --> 00:09:18,050
about three point six billion dollars of free cash flow.

138
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:23,293
And Tesla is now spending even more heavily on its next bets.

139
00:09:23,293 --> 00:09:26,122
In the second quarter of 2026 alone,

140
00:09:26,122 --> 00:09:30,994
its capital spending reached five point eight billion dollars.

141
00:09:30,994 --> 00:09:36,651
That quarter, its free cash flow, the cash left after those investments,

142
00:09:36,651 --> 00:09:40,580
was negative: minus one point one billion dollars.

143
00:09:41,030 --> 00:09:43,714
Revenue that quarter was actually a record:

144
00:09:43,714 --> 00:09:46,960
twenty-eight billion dollars, up twenty-six percent.

145
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:50,705
But net profit was one point one billion, and more than half

146
00:09:50,705 --> 00:09:54,325
of that came from a five-hundred-and-ninety-million-dollar

147
00:09:54,325 --> 00:09:58,070
gain outside its normal business, not from selling anything.

148
00:09:59,170 --> 00:10:04,057
If you only looked at these numbers, you'd see a large, profitable carmaker,

149
00:10:04,057 --> 00:10:07,850
with slowing car sales and a fast-growing battery business.

150
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:10,860
That's not how Tesla's shareholders see it.

151
00:10:11,310 --> 00:10:16,642
In November 2025, more than seventy-five percent of them voted for a new pay

152
00:10:16,642 --> 00:10:22,114
plan for Elon Musk, potentially worth up to a trillion dollars over ten years.

153
00:10:22,114 --> 00:10:25,482
It was opposed by Norway's sovereign wealth fund

154
00:10:25,482 --> 00:10:28,990
and by the two biggest shareholder advisory firms.

155
00:10:29,440 --> 00:10:33,954
What matters for us is what Musk has to deliver to get paid.

156
00:10:33,954 --> 00:10:38,619
The plan lists the targets: twenty million vehicles delivered.

157
00:10:38,619 --> 00:10:43,434
Ten million subscriptions to Tesla's driver-assistance software.

158
00:10:43,434 --> 00:10:45,541
One million humanoid robots.

159
00:10:45,541 --> 00:10:48,851
One million robotaxis in commercial service.

160
00:10:48,851 --> 00:10:50,055
A profit target.

161
00:10:50,055 --> 00:10:54,720
And a stock market value of eight and a half trillion dollars.

162
00:10:55,170 --> 00:10:56,854
Look at that list again.

163
00:10:56,854 --> 00:10:59,239
Cars are only one item out of six.

164
00:10:59,239 --> 00:11:03,308
Three of them are about software and machines that, today,

165
00:11:03,308 --> 00:11:08,850
bring in little revenue: driver-assistance subscriptions, robotaxis and robots.

166
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:12,178
In other words, the shareholders who approved this plan

167
00:11:12,178 --> 00:11:15,004
aren't mainly paying for the Tesla we just took apart.

168
00:11:15,004 --> 00:11:17,620
They're paying for a Tesla that doesn't exist yet.

169
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:20,680
So, how does Tesla actually make money?

170
00:11:21,130 --> 00:11:24,929
Mostly by selling cars, at thinner margins than before,

171
00:11:24,929 --> 00:11:29,972
in a market where it's no longer the biggest seller of electric vehicles.

172
00:11:29,972 --> 00:11:33,703
Increasingly, by selling batteries to power companies.

173
00:11:33,703 --> 00:11:36,466
Steadily, through charging and services.

174
00:11:36,466 --> 00:11:40,403
And, for years, thanks to billions of dollars paid by its

175
00:11:40,403 --> 00:11:44,410
own competitors, a source of profit that is now shrinking.

176
00:11:44,860 --> 00:11:47,850
Whether Tesla is worth what investors pay for it depends

177
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:50,787
on something that doesn't appear in these accounts yet.

178
00:11:50,787 --> 00:11:53,136
That's not a question we can answer for you.

179
00:11:53,136 --> 00:11:56,500
But now you know exactly what's in the numbers, and what isn't.
